 "modules": [
  {
   "id": "il08-mit-63",
   "code": "IL-08",
   "title": "MIT 6-3: The Distilled Core",
   "summary": "Welcome to IL-08 — Algorithms, Compilers, Distributed & Formal — and to Module 1: MIT's EECS curriculum, distilled to prerequisite order. I'm your instructor, and over the next three modules we're going to build the deep core that never expires: proofs, algorithms, the machine, and the limits of computation. In this module we follow MIT 6-3's own sequence — 6.100A/B Python (computational thinking and problem-set discipline), 6.1200 Mathematics for Computer Science (proofs, induction, number theory, graphs, probability), 6.1210/6.1220 Algorithms (design, analysis, lower bounds, NP-completeness), 6.1910 Computation Structures (gates to processor, the hardware/software contract), 6.1800/6.1810 Systems Engineering (modularity, the end-to-end argument, the xv6 kernel), and theory from automata to complexity (the 6.1400 lineage). No algorithm in this module without an induction proof of it; no system without a machine model beneath it. Let's begin with the mathematician's toolkit.",
   "objectives": [
    "Write proofs by induction, contradiction, and invariant.",
    "Analyze algorithms: recurrences, amortized analysis, lower bounds.",
    "Explain the hardware/software contract: ISA, assembly, caches, VM.",
    "State what NP-completeness means and prove a reduction."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **induction** — proof by base case, inductive hypothesis, and inductive step; every recursive algorithm's correctness proof uses it — the template until it's reflex\n- **loop invariant** — a property true before and after each iteration; the loop invariant proves the loop — state it before writing the loop\n- **contradiction** — assume the negation of the claim, derive an absurdity; the halting proof's weapon\n- **number theory** — divisibility, modular arithmetic, gcd; Fermat's little theorem and Euler's theorem — RSA's prerequisites, straight from 6.1200\n- **graph theory** — the universal modeling language: vertices, edges, BFS/DFS, Dijkstra, MST\n- **divide-and-conquer** — split, solve recursively, combine; analyzed via recurrences like T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n)\n- **master theorem** — closed form for T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n): compare f(n) against n^(log_b a) — smaller gives case 1, equal gives case 2 (times log n), larger (regularity) gives case 3; memorize the three cases, derive when unsure\n- **amortized analysis** — cost averaged over a sequence of operations; three methods: aggregate, accounting, potential — dynamic-array append is O(1) amortized via doubling\n- **exchange argument** — the proof pattern for greedy correctness: show an optimal solution can be transformed to include the greedy choice — greedy stays ahead (MST, scheduling)\n- **lower bound** — a proof that no algorithm in a model can do better; comparison sorting is Ω(n log n) by the decision-tree argument: n! leaves need log(n!) depth\n- **reduction** — to prove X hard, reduce a known-hard problem TO X in polynomial time; reduction direction matters (3-SAT ≤p X proves X hard)\n- **NP-completeness** — the hardest problems in NP; 3-SAT is the canonical one — reductions start here\n- **FSM** — finite-state machine: combinational logic plus state; the building block of the FSM-to-processor climb — every level is the previous level's abstraction, no magic\n- **hardware/software contract** — the ISA, assembly, and calling conventions: the exact boundary the hardware promises the software\n- **end-to-end argument** — implement correctness-critical functions at the endpoints, not in the network; the network can't guarantee what endpoints need\n- **xv6** — MIT's teaching Unix-like kernel in ~10k lines; reading a real kernel demystifies scheduling, virtual memory, and filesystems\n- **diagonalization** — self-referential construction used to prove the halting problem undecidable; the most beautiful proof in CS — learn it cold\n- **P vs NP** — P: solvable in polynomial time; NP: verifiable in polynomial time; NP-complete problems are the hardest in NP; PSPACE sits beyond"
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Prove and analyze an algorithm, end to end — the procedure behind the Induction Proof Workshop and the recurrence drills:\n1. **State the claim precisely** — the specification: what must hold, for which inputs (e.g., sum_{i=1..n}(2i-1) = n^2).\n2. **Write the loop invariant before the loop** — a property true before and after each iteration (binary search: the key, if present, lies in a[lo..hi)).\n3. **Induction skeleton: BASE → HYPOTHESIS → STEP** — prove the base case, assume the hypothesis for k, derive k+1 (Bernoulli step: (1+x)^(k+1) = (1+x)^k(1+x) ≥ (1+kx)(1+x) = 1+(k+1)x+kx² ≥ 1+(k+1)x).\n4. **Preserve the invariant each iteration** — binary search halves [lo,hi): if key < a[mid] set hi=mid, else lo=mid+1; the key stays inside; hi−lo shrinks, so the loop terminates.\n5. **Write the recurrence** — count the work per level (mergesort: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n).\n6. **Apply the master theorem** — T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n is case 2: f(n) = n = n^(log_2 2), so Θ(n log n).\n7. **Check amortized cost where operations repeat** — dynamic-array append with doubling: n appends cost O(n) total, O(1) amortized each.\n8. **Argue the lower bound** — is your bound tight? Comparison sorting cannot beat Ω(n log n).\n9. **Classify the problem** — in P, or NP-complete? If the latter (reduce 3-SAT to it, show membership in NP), stop hunting for the perfect algorithm and start approximating."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your algorithm dossier — the capstone for the MIT distilled core. Pick one algorithm from the canon: quicksort, Dijkstra's shortest path, or the dynamic-array append.\nThe dossier has four artifacts, and each is graded:\n1. **Implementation** — correct Python code (BFS-style clarity: the code from the BFS lab is the bar).\n2. **Correctness proof** — written in the BASE/HYPOTHESIS/STEP skeleton with the loop invariant stated up front, exactly like the Induction Proof Workshop.\n3. **Running-time analysis** — the recurrence plus its master-theorem solution (or amortized argument for the dynamic array), with a tightness verdict from the lower-bound side.\n4. **Complexity verdict** — one paragraph: where does this algorithm's problem sit in the complexity landscape (P? NP-complete? undecidable-adjacent?), and what changes if the input grows 100×?\nThese four artifacts — code, proof, analysis, verdict — are what a theory-literate engineer produces for any algorithm they ship."
    },
    {
     "title": "Noah check-in",
     "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- In one sentence, what does the master theorem decide for a recurrence — and which case is mergesort?\n- State the binary-search loop invariant, then prove it is preserved when you set hi = mid.\n- You claim problem X is NP-complete. Reduce 3-SAT to X — and defend why the reduction goes in that direction.\n- Walk me through diagonalization: why can't a halting decider exist?\n- The end-to-end argument says reliable delivery belongs at the endpoints. Give me one more endpoint-vs-network example from real systems.\n- Honesty note: these proofs, labs, and questions are original practice material authored for this course. Course numbers reference public university materials; this course confers no certificate and is not affiliated with MIT, CMU, or Stanford."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Prove: sum of first n odds = n². Method?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Prove: sum of first n odds = n². Method?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** induction\n- **Why:** Induction: base n=1, assume n, show n+1 adds (2n+1): n²+2n+1=(n+1)².\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Induction: base n=1, assume n, show n+1 adds (2n+1): n²+2n+1=(n+1)².* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Formal material demands rigor. These habits corrupt understanding of MIT 6-3: The Distilled Core:\n- **Hand-waving proofs.** 'Clearly' and 'obviously' are where the bugs hide. Write the invariant, the base case, and the step — fully, every time.\n- **Ignoring edge cases in analysis.** Empty input, single element, duplicates, maximum size. The analysis isn't done until the edges are accounted for.\n- **Confusing average with worst case.** Know which one your bound describes and which one your adversary gets to choose.\n- **Notation sloppiness.** O, Theta, Omega mean different things; using them interchangeably trains fuzzy thinking. Say exactly which bound you proved.\n- **Skipping the reduction.** Every new problem in MIT 6-3: The Distilled Core should be connected to something known: 'this is just X in disguise.' Reductions are how the field thinks.\n- **Code without the argument.** An implementation with no written correctness or complexity argument is half a solution. The argument is the deliverable; code is the exhibit."
    }
   ],
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Induction template?",
     "back": "Base case → inductive hypothesis → inductive step. Reflex."
    },
    {
     "front": "Loop invariant?",
     "back": "A property true before/after each iteration — proves the loop correct. State it first."
    },
    {
     "front": "Master theorem cases?",
     "back": "f(n) vs n^(log_b a): smaller → case 1; equal → case 2 (× log n); larger (regular) → case 3."
    },
    {
     "front": "Comparison sorting lower bound?",
     "back": "Ω(n log n) — decision-tree argument: n! leaves need log(n!) depth."
    },
    {
     "front": "Reduction direction for hardness?",
     "back": "Reduce known-hard TO your problem (3-SAT ≤p X proves X hard). Direction matters."
    },
    {
     "front": "Halting problem proof technique?",
     "back": "Diagonalization — assume a decider, construct the contradiction."
    },
    {
     "front": "Amortized analysis: the idea?",
     "back": "Average cost per operation over a sequence — dynamic arrays double on overflow: O(1) amortized push despite O(n) worst case."
    },
    {
     "front": "Cache's lesson for algorithms?",
     "back": "Locality beats asymptotics at real sizes — sequential scans win over pointer-chasing even when Big-O ties."
    },
    {
     "front": "Prove: sum of first n odds = n². Method?",
     "back": "**induction** — Induction: base n=1, assume n, show n+1 adds (2n+1): n²+2n+1=(n+1)²."
    },
    {
     "front": "T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n. Solution?",
     "back": "**O(n log n)** — Master case 2: f(n)=n = n^(log_2 2) → Θ(n log n). Mergesort's recurrence."
    },
    {
     "front": "Amortized cost of dynamic-array append?",
     "back": "**O(1)** — Doubling strategy: n appends cost O(n) total — O(1) amortized each."
    },
    {
     "front": "To prove problem X NP-complete, you?",
     "back": "**reduce 3-SAT to X in polytime + show X in NP** — 3-SAT ≤p X shows X at least as hard; X ∈ NP completes it."
    }
   ],
