  {
   "id": "il08-stanford-cs",
   "code": "IL-08",
   "title": "Stanford: The Core Cross-Walk",
   "summary": "Welcome to Module 3 — Stanford's core as a cross-walk. I'm your instructor, and this module is about triangulation: we walk the same ground the MIT and CMU modules covered, but in Stanford's order, so that what survives all three presentations is the concept itself. 106A's methodology in Python, 106B's abstractions in C++ with recursion as the core skill, CS103's proofs, logic, and graphs, CS109's probability for computer scientists, CS107's computer organization and systems (where pointers stop being scary), CS111's operating systems principles, and CS161's famously rigorous design and analysis — with randomization as the signature move. We close with the depth branches: theory (154/254), systems (143 compilers, 144 networking, 145 databases), and the AI sequence. Same ideas, third telling — keep what's invariant.",
   "objectives": [
    "Think recursively: 106B's core skill — recursion as a problem-solving strategy.",
    "Prove and count: logic, graphs, probability (103/109).",
    "Program the machine: bits, C, assembly, OS principles (107/111).",
    "Analyze: CS161's algorithm canon with randomization."
   ],
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **recursion-first** — think recursively, then convert to iterative; 106B's core problem-solving strategy — recursive problem-solving before iterative\n- **big-O** — asymptotic upper bound on growth; 106B's language for comparing algorithms\n- **expectation** — the average over randomness; a fair die expects 3.5 — linearity of expectation starts here\n- **linearity of expectation** — E[X+Y] = E[X] + E[Y] with no independence needed; the analysis workhorse for randomized algorithms\n- **randomized algorithm** — uses random choices; randomized quicksort runs in expected O(n log n) — randomization defeats adversarial input\n- **amplification** — repeat a randomized algorithm; failure probability drops exponentially — Karger repeated n² log n times yields the min cut with high probability\n- **Karger's min-cut** — randomized edge contraction; the algorithm that's wrong sometimes and brilliant in expectation — repeat for confidence\n- **compilation pipeline** — lex → parse → semantic analysis → IR → optimization → codegen: tokens to tree to checked IR to machine code\n- **RAII** — resource acquisition is initialization; 106B's C++ idiom for managing lifetimes — the language where abstractions cost nothing\n- **buffer overflow** — writing past a buffer's bounds; CS107's assignments make memory unsafety visceral — security from the machine up\n- **CS109's probability** — expectation, variance, concentration: the subset of probability a computer scientist actually uses (randomized algorithms, ML)\n- **cross-walk value** — see the same ideas (recursion, induction, layering) across MIT, CMU, and Stanford; triangulation — the idea outlives any single presentation\n- **depth branch** — a specialization track: theory (154 automata/complexity, 254 advanced), systems (143 compilers, 144 networking, 145 databases), AI (221, 229, 230, 224N, 231N, 234 — the sequence IL-07's ML arc follows)"
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Worked estimates — CS109-style: put numbers on randomized claims before shipping them:\n1. **Expected quicksort** — n = 1,000,000, random pivot: expected ≈ n log₂ n ≈ 20M comparisons. Worst case without randomization: ≈ n²/2 = 5×10¹¹. Randomization's payoff, quantified — this is why the pivot is random.\n2. **Amplification arithmetic** — one Karger run succeeds with probability ≈ 2/n². Repeat n² log n times: failure ≈ (1 − 2/n²)^(n² log n) ≈ e^(−2 log n) = 1/n². Repetition drives the error down exponentially — high probability from a weak primitive.\n3. **Linearity of expectation** — expected sum of 100 fair dice = 100 × 3.5 = 350. No distribution math, no independence assumptions — linearity always holds.\n4. **Hashing load estimate** — n keys into m = n buckets, uniform hashing: expected chain length = n/m = 1. Double the keys (n = 2m): expected length 2. This is the estimate behind every hash-table sizing decision."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Randomization, proven and shipped — the capstone for the Stanford cross-walk. Implement randomized quicksort and Karger's min-cut in Python:\n1. **Working code** — both algorithms, clean enough to hand to a reviewer.\n2. **Expectation analysis** — written in prose, no hand-waving: show the linearity-of-expectation step for quicksort's O(n log n) and the 2/n² success bound for Karger.\n3. **Empirical check** — run quicksort on adversarial (sorted) input 50 times, record the comparison counts, and report the distribution (min/median/max); then run Karger's min-cut repeatedly on a small graph and record how many runs find the true min cut — does the amplification arithmetic from the estimation section match?\n4. **Production verdict** — one paragraph: when would you reach for randomization in production, and when is determinism non-negotiable?\nDeliverables: code, analysis, measurements, verdict. Then write the compiler-phase map for a language you know: which phase would catch a type error, which would catch a syntax error, and where does optimization live — the 143 pipeline made concrete."
    },
    {
     "title": "Noah check-in",
     "body": "Noah probes mastery with questions like:\n- Randomized quicksort on sorted input: expected time, and why does the random pivot defeat the adversary?\n- Karger succeeds with probability ≈ 2/n² per run. How many repeats for high probability — and show the amplification arithmetic.\n- Walk the compiler pipeline for `x = 1 +` — which phase rejects it, and why not an earlier one?\n- Expected sum of 100 fair dice — no calculator, just linearity. Then tell me why independence wasn't needed.\n- You've now seen induction at MIT, CMU, and Stanford. What's invariant across all three — and what did each school add?\n- Honesty note: these proofs, labs, and questions are original practice material authored for this course. Course numbers reference public university materials; this course confers no certificate and is not affiliated with MIT, CMU, or Stanford."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Randomized quicksort expected time?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Randomized quicksort expected time?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** O(n log n)\n- **Why:** Random pivot → expected O(n log n) — randomization defeats adversarial input.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Random pivot → expected O(n log n) — randomization defeats adversarial input.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Formal material demands rigor. These habits corrupt understanding of Stanford: The Core Cross-Walk:\n- **Hand-waving proofs.** 'Clearly' and 'obviously' are where the bugs hide. Write the invariant, the base case, and the step — fully, every time.\n- **Ignoring edge cases in analysis.** Empty input, single element, duplicates, maximum size. The analysis isn't done until the edges are accounted for.\n- **Confusing average with worst case.** Know which one your bound describes and which one your adversary gets to choose.\n- **Notation sloppiness.** O, Theta, Omega mean different things; using them interchangeably trains fuzzy thinking. Say exactly which bound you proved.\n- **Skipping the reduction.** Every new problem in Stanford: The Core Cross-Walk should be connected to something known: 'this is just X in disguise.' Reductions are how the field thinks.\n- **Code without the argument.** An implementation with no written correctness or complexity argument is half a solution. The argument is the deliverable; code is the exhibit."
    }
   ],
