 "answerBank": [
  {
   "q": "Which school's version should I follow?",
   "a": "All three where they overlap — that's the point of the cross-walk. Start with MIT's order (prerequisite-clean), use CMU for systems depth (15-213/410), Stanford for the AI branches. The module sequences them.",
   "ref": "il08-mit-63"
  },
  {
   "q": "Do I really need theory for a dev job?",
   "a": "For most CRUD work, no. For interviews at selective companies, distributed systems, databases, and performance work — yes, constantly. Theory is the highest-leverage knowledge: it doesn't expire.",
   "ref": "il08-cmu-cs"
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I learn proofs as a programmer?",
   "a": "Write them like code: state the claim (spec), then the proof (implementation). Do every exercise in 6.1200/CS103 — proofs are a writing skill, and writing is learned by writing.",
   "ref": "il08-mit-63"
  },
  {
   "q": "xv6 vs Linux for learning OS?",
   "a": "xv6 first (10k lines, every concept visible), then Linux source for the specific subsystem you need. The module uses xv6 as the reference implementation.",
   "ref": "il08-mit-63"
  },
  {
   "q": "P vs NP — should I care?",
   "a": "As a practitioner: NP-completeness tells you when to stop searching for the perfect algorithm and start approximating. That's a career's worth of saved time in one concept.",
   "ref": "il08-mit-63"
  }
 ],
